{
  "raw": {
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283880": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor of Philosophy Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should submit the following materials through I nterfolio: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement Holistic Engagement Statement (optional) Names of three professional references Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. All applications must be submitted via Interfolio. Job #193096. Applications submitted through careers.umich.edu will not be reviewed. Who We Are Campus and Community Information The University of Michigan-Dearborn was founded in 1959 on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate in Dearborn, Michigan. As one of the University of Michigan's three campuses, we serve over 8,000 students and provide high-quality undergraduate, graduate, professional, and continuing education programs that embody the prestige of the University of Michigan name. Our mission is rooted in a commitment to Metro Detroit's well-being and diversity, fostering a caring, dynamic, intellectual, and socially welcoming environment. UM-Dearborn utilizes the Boyer Model of academic scholarship. We distinguish ourselves by building and nurturing intentional, mutually beneficial partnerships with local industries, governments, and community organizations. Our faculty are recognized for their excellence in scholarship and teaching and are actively engaged in professional and academic service. UM-Dearborn offers ample opportunities for creative, collaborative research, practice-based learning, and community engagement through scholarly and educational partnerships. Faculty have the privilege of working closely with their students, 47% of whom are first-generation college students, 46% are Pell Grant-eligible, and 31% are students of color. The average class size is 25 students, and 69% of classes have fewer than 30 students. U.S. News & amp; World Report consistently ranks UM-Dearborn among the best regional universities in the Midwest. In 2024, UM-Dearborn ranked the number-one regional public university in Michigan and third in the Midwest. The university is also recognized on Forbes; list of America's Top Colleges. For more information, please visit our website at www.umdearborn.edu . Dearborn is a vibrant, diverse community centrally located within one of America?s largest business regions. The area provides a variety of urban, suburban, and rural environments within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, its suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Job Summary The Philosophy Discipline in the Department of Language, Culture, and the Arts, College of Arts, Sciences, and Letters (CASL) at the University of Michigan-Dearborn invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Philosophy, with an area of specialization in Philosophy of Artificial Intelligence and an area of competence in Philosophy of Science or Philosophy of Mind. The preferred starting date is September 2027. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Philosophy by the time of appointment and demonstrate strong potential for excellence in research and teaching. The Philosophy Discipline is committed to excellence in undergraduate education and scholarship. Philosophy serves students from across the university through high-enrollment foundational courses as well as upper-level courses in a broad range of philosophical areas. The successful candidate will contribute to the continued development of the Philosophy curriculum, particularly in Philosophy of Artificial Intelligence and related areas, and will have opportunities for interdisciplinary engagement with programs and faculty across the university. Teaching responsibilities are based on a 2-2 teaching load and will include courses in the candidate's areas of specialization and competence, as well as foundational Philosophy courses such as Introduction to Philosophy, Critical Thinking, and Ethics. The successful candidate will be expected to develop and teach courses in Philosophy of Artificial Intelligence and related areas, which may include AI Ethics and Philosophy of Technology. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active program of scholarly research appropriate to a tenure-track appointment and to contribute service to the Philosophy Discipline, the Department of Language, Culture, and the Arts, the College, the University, and the profession. This is an on-site position, and the successful candidate will be expected to teach in a variety of modalities, including in-person, hybrid, and online courses. Required Qualifications* Ph.D. in Philosophy completed by the time of appointment. Area of specialization in Philosophy of Artificial Intelligence. Area of competence in Philosophy of Science or Philosophy of Mind. Evidence of, or strong potential for, excellence in philosophical research. Evidence of, or strong potential for, excellence in undergraduate teaching. Ability to teach foundational undergraduate Philosophy courses, including courses such as Introduction to Philosophy, Critical Thinking, and Ethics. Ability to develop and teach courses in Philosophy of Artificial Intelligence and related areas. Desired Qualifications* Demonstrated potential for interdisciplinary teaching, research, or collaboration involving artificial intelligence and related areas. Experience with, or interest in, developing connections between Philosophy and other academic disciplines or programs. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283880 Working Title Assistant Professor of Philosophy Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Arts Sci Ltr Department Dbn CASL-Language Culture Arts Posting Begin/End Date 10/01/2026 - 12/30/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283156": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Geropsychology/Psychology of Aging",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Geropsychology/Psychology of Aging | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor in Geropsychology/Psychology of Aging Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should send a letter of intent, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching philosophy and experience, evidence of teaching performance, a statement of research experience and interest, samples of representative papers, and three letters of recommendation. All applications must be submitted via Interfolio by December 30, 2026; applications submitted through careers.umich.edu will not be reviewed. The review of applications will begin on October 26, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. If you have questions about this position, application requirements, or the application process, please email Nitya Sethuraman at [email protected] . Who We Are The University of Michigan-Dearborn was founded in 1959 on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate in Dearborn, Michigan. As one of the University of Michigan's three campuses, we serve over 8,000 students and provide high-quality undergraduate, graduate, professional, and continuing education programs that embody the prestige of the University of Michigan name. Our mission is rooted in a commitment to Metro Detroit's well-being and diversity, fostering a caring, dynamic, intellectual, and socially welcoming environment. UM-Dearborn utilizes the Boyer Model of academic scholarship. We distinguish ourselves by building and nurturing intentional, mutually beneficial partnerships with local industries, governments, and community organizations. Our faculty are recognized for their excellence in scholarship and teaching and are actively engaged in professional and academic service. UM-Dearborn offers ample opportunities for creative, collaborative research, practice-based learning, and community engagement through scholarly and educational partnerships. Faculty have the privilege of working closely with their students, 47% of whom are first-generation college students, 46% are Pell Grant-eligible, and 31% are students of color. The average class size is 25 students, and 69% of classes have fewer than 30 students. U.S. News & World Report consistently ranks UM-Dearborn among the best regional universities in the Midwest. In 2024, UM-Dearborn ranked the number-one regional public university in Michigan and third in the Midwest. The university is also recognized on Forbes' list of America's Top Colleges. For more information, please visit our website at www.umdearborn.edu. Dearborn is a vibrant, diverse community centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The area provides a variety of urban, suburban, and rural environments within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, its suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Job Summary The Psychology Discipline within the Department of Behavioral Sciences in the College of Arts, Sciences, and Letters (CASL) invites applications from well-qualified candidates for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in geropsychology/psychology of aging. Candidates for this position must have earned a Ph.D. in developmental psychology, geropsychology, or a related area by August 1, 2027. The starting date is August 30, 2027. This is an on-site position. The successful candidate will be expected to teach in multiple modalities, including in-person, online, and hybrid. The Department of Behavioral Sciences is interdisciplinary, bringing together several fields focused on understanding human behavior and social processes. The department has a diverse faculty comprising specialists in psychology, sociology, anthropology, and related interdisciplinary fields. Psychology is one of the largest majors on campus, with 14 tenure-track faculty members. In addition to the undergraduate major, the discipline offers a master?s program in clinical health psychology. Faculty in the discipline are interested in a variety of topics including cognitive psychology, neuroscience, social psychology, health/wellbeing, clinical psychology, and developmental psychology. In addition, faculty in the department have established collaborations with other units/departments on campus, including biology, public health, computer science, and human-centered design. The successful candidate will support both Psychology and the interdisciplinary Behavioral and Biological Sciences program and will be expected to teach at both the graduate and undergraduate levels. Tenure-track faculty teach a 2-2 course load. The successful candidate will be expected to teach Psychology of Aging, Lab in Developmental Psychology, and additional courses such as Introductory Psychology, Lifespan Development, Introduction to Developmental Psychology, or courses in their area of specialization. Candidates should also be skilled at working with undergraduate and graduate students for research, mentoring, and supervision. Candidates with a focus on cognition (e.g., a specialty in cognitive aging or aging neuroscience) or health-related research on aging (e.g., individual experiences of aging or later life psychosocial processes related to health) are highly encouraged to apply, but other areas of research on psychology of aging will also be fully considered. Required Qualifications* A Ph.D. in Developmental Psychology, Geropsychology, or a closely related field, with demonstrated expertise in aging. Evidence of teaching excellence and research potential is required. A willingness to support and advise undergraduate and graduate research is also expected. In addition to research and teaching duties, candidates will be expected to serve within the discipline, department, college, campus, and broader field. Desired Qualifications* A record of research productivity, a clear research agenda, evidence of teaching effectiveness at the university level, and a commitment to fostering an inclusive and diverse learning environment. Experience in securing or potential for securing external grant funding is highly desirable. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283156 Working Title Assistant Professor in Geropsychology/Psychology of Aging Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Arts Sci Ltr Department Dbn CASL-Psychology Posting Begin/End Date 9/16/2026 - 12/30/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284041": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I HCED 380/450",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I HCED 380/450 - IMSE | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I HCED 380/450 - IMSE Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants must submit all required materials through the official U-M Careers portal (careers.umich.edu): Curriculum vitae (CV) or resume Cover letter describing relevant engineering experience, teaching philosophy, and familiarity with lab equipment Contact information for 2-3 professional references The deadline to apply is October 17, 2026. Applications will be reviewed beginning immediately. This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the academic unit. The expected date by which the offer of appointment will be made, starting in Winter 2027, Jan 6. Job Summary About the Role: The Department of Industrial and Manufacturing Systems Engineering (IMSE) in the College of Engineering and Computer Science (CECS) at the University of Michigan-Dearborn invites applications for a part-time Lecturer to teach Human-Centered Engineering Design. The lecturer is expected to teach HCED 380 during Winter terms and HCED 450 during Fall terms on a rotating basis. Each course includes three credits of lecture and one credit of laboratory instruction. Specific assignments are subject to curricular need, enrollment, and final unit approval. This role bridges classroom instruction with hands-on lab experimentation, guiding students through the full iterative lifecycle of rapid prototyping, fabrication, and product development. The ideal candidate brings substantial industrial product development experience and a passion for mentoring student engineers in modern, technology- enabled maker space and lab environments. Key Responsibilities: Instruction and Classroom Delivery: Serve as the primary instructor for HCED 380 or HCED 450, according to the department?s course rotation. Prepare and deliver lectures, syllabi, laboratory instruction, and course materials covering foundational and advanced concepts in product development and physical prototyping. Hands-On Lab Instruction &amp; Supervision: Provide practical, in-lab instruction and safety supervision on rapid prototyping tools and manufacturing equipment, including FDM and SLA 3D printing, laser etching, waterjet cutting, and microcontroller integration. Student Project Mentorship: Direct, advise, and evaluate undergraduate design projects, guiding students through technical troubleshooting, project scoping, and engineering design workflows. Lab Management and Supply Coordination: Assist with day-to-day operation and scheduling of lab facilities, identify required components and project consumables, and coordinate equipment maintenance in support of student projects. Curriculum and Safety Compliance: Collaborate with department faculty to update course content in line with emerging industry standards. Enforce safety protocols and compliance across all departmental prototyping spaces. Assessment and Evaluation: Grade coursework, evaluate project milestones, hold regular office hours, and track student learning outcomes in accordance with U-M standards. Required Qualifications* Education: Master's degree or Ph.D. in Engineering (Industrial & Manufacturing, Mechanical, Systems, Design, or a closely related field) from an accredited institution. Engineering Experience: At least 5 years of professional engineering experience in industry or applied research. Product Development: At least 3 years of hands-on experience across the product development lifecycle. Project Management: Demonstrated working knowledge of project management methodologies. Communication: Exceptional oral, written, and interpersonal communication skills, with a proven ability to engage diverse undergraduate audiences. Desired Qualifications* Additive Manufacturing: Hands-on expertise with FDM and SLA 3D printing platforms; experience with Formlabs and Bambu Lab printers strongly preferred. Microcontrollers & Embedded Hardware: Proficiency with microcontroller prototyping platforms, particularly the Arduino ecosystem. Fabrication Technologies: Practical experience operating and troubleshooting waterjet cutters and laser etchers/engravers. Intellectual Property: Familiarity with invention methodologies and intellectual property, including patents, copyrights, trademarks, and trade secrets. Teaching Experience: Prior college-level instruction, lab instruction, or technical workforce training in engineering design or prototyping. Additional Information The University of Michigan-Dearborn (UM-Dearborn) is one of the three campuses of the University of Michigan. UM-Dearborn, a comprehensive university offering high quality undergraduate, graduate, professional and continuing education to residents of southeastern Michigan, and attracts nearly 9,000 students. Our faculty come from respected universities and doctoral programs, are recognized for excellence in research and teaching, and are active in professional and academic service roles in their respective fields. US News and World Report recently recognized our campus as a Best Regional University. The campus is located on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate. Dearborn is centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The geographically diverse area provides faculty with a variety of urban, suburban, and rural areas within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, Detroit suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284041 Working Title LEO Lecturer I HCED 380/450 - IMSE Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Engineering Department Dbn Coll of Eng-Mechanical Eng Posting Begin/End Date 10/07/2026 - 10/17/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284644": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I - ESE 510",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - ESE 510 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - ESE 510 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Extended Site Maintenance We are performing updates and maintenance to our applicant experience. As a result, the site will be unavailable Saturday, October 31st at 9pm EST through Sunday, November 1st at 8:00am EST. During this outage period, applications for job postings can not be submitted. Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is recommended for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Please also include evidence of teaching excellence and relevant recommendations. The application deadline is November 6, 2026. The review of applications will start immediately, with an appointment offer expected by November 11, 2026. This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the academic unit. Job Summary The Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering at UM-Dearborn is seeking a Lecturer I for Winter Term 2027 to support ESE 510-001 \"Sustainability Science and Engineering\" in-person and ESE 510-002 web instruction. This appointment starts on January 1, 2027 and runs through April 30, 2027. This course incorporates ethical and social issues into the design of products, processes and practices that will benefit the society as a whole. Specific emphasis will be given to engineering principles that will help engineers design products and services to meet societal needs with minimal impact on the global ecosystem. Using specific examples and illustrations, the course will demonstrate opportunities for sustainable engineering practices, providing students with valuable insight to applying these principles. Responsibilities* The Lecturer I will be expected to support ESE 510-001 \"Sustainability Science and Engineering\" in-person and ESE 510-002 web instruction for Winter Term 2027 . T he co urse schedule for the Winter 2027 term is Thursdays 6:00-8:45pm. This is expected to be a 25% appointment. Specific activities include course preparation with syllabus, content delivery, grading, office hours, discussion/meetings with the Department Chair. Required Qualifications* Candidates will possess, at a minimum, a Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Environmental Science or Geography with relevant specialization, relevant teaching experience and evidence of teaching excellence. Candidates will be evaluated based on submitted CV, teaching evaluations, and relevant recommendations. Desired Qualifications* None beyond the minimum qualifications listed above. Why Work at U-M Dearborn? The University of Michigan-Dearborn (UM-Dearborn) is one of the three campuses of the University of Michigan. UM-Dearborn, a comprehensive university offering high quality undergraduate, graduate, professional and continuing education to residents of southeastern Michigan, and attracts more than 9,000 students. Our faculty comes from respected universities and doctoral programs, are recognized for excellence in research and teaching, and are active in professional and academic service roles in their respective fields. US News and World Report has consistently ranked UM-Dearborn among the best universities in the Midwest and a top-ranked regional university in Michigan. The campus is located on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate. Dearborn is centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The geographically diverse area provides faculty with a variety of urban, suburban, and rural areas within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, Detroit suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candid ates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks. Background checks will be performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284644 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - ESE 510 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Hybrid Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Engineering Department Dbn Col of Eng-Electric & Comp Posting Begin/End Date 10/27/2026 - 11/06/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284637": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I - ESE 505",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - ESE 505 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - ESE 505 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Extended Site Maintenance We are performing updates and maintenance to our applicant experience. As a result, the site will be unavailable Saturday, October 31st at 9pm EST through Sunday, November 1st at 8:00am EST. During this outage period, applications for job postings can not be submitted. Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is recommended for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Please also include evidence of teaching excellence and relevant recommendations. The application deadline is November 6, 2026. The review of applications will start immediately, with an appointment offer expected by November 11, 2026. This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the academic unit. Job Summary The Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering at UM-Dearborn is seeking a Lecturer I for Winter Term 2027 to support ESE 505-001 \"Ecosystem Engineering and Management\" in-person and ESE 505-002 web instruction. This appointment starts on January 1, 2027 and runs through April 30, 2027. The course is designed to provide principles of ecological engineering and design of ecosystems that integrate human society with its natural environment for the benefit of both. Particular emphasis is placed on 1) Understand and describe important physical, chemical, and biological processes that affect ecosystem integrity; 2) Explain, use, and illustrate mass balances, water balances, energy balances, and chemical balances as tools to help understand and describe ecosystem functions; 3) Familiarity with approaches to ecological restoration of streams and rivers, wetlands and riparian areas, lakes and reservoirs, and coastal ecosystems; 4) Understand and apply concepts involved in designing and achieving sustainable uses of ecosystems (e.g., treatment wetlands and land treatment systems), and application of ecological engineering techniques in stormwater management; 5) Analyze and illustrate the impact that designing ecosystems to solve engineering problems has in the context of societal and global issues; and 6) Identify and describe the contemporary issues and emerging fields in which ecological engineers will be called upon to use their expertise. Responsibilities* The Lecturer I will be expected to support ESE 505-001 \"Ecosystem Engineering and Management \" in-person and ESE 505-002 web instruction for Winter Term 2027 . T he co urse schedule for the Winter 2027 term is Tuesdays 6:00-8:45pm. This is expected to be a 25% appointment. Specific activities include course preparation with syllabus, content delivery, grading, office hours, discussion/meetings with the Department Chair. Required Qualifications* Candidates will possess, at a minimum, a Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Environmental Science or Geography with relevant specialization, relevant teaching experience and evidence of teaching excellence. Candidates will be evaluated based on submitted CV, teaching evaluations, and relevant recommendations. Desired Qualifications* None beyond the minimum qualifications listed above. Why Work at U-M Dearborn? The University of Michigan-Dearborn (UM-Dearborn) is one of the three campuses of the University of Michigan. UM-Dearborn, a comprehensive university offering high quality undergraduate, graduate, professional and continuing education to residents of southeastern Michigan, and attracts more than 9,000 students. Our faculty comes from respected universities and doctoral programs, are recognized for excellence in research and teaching, and are active in professional and academic service roles in their respective fields. US News and World Report has consistently ranked UM-Dearborn among the best universities in the Midwest and a top-ranked regional university in Michigan. The campus is located on 200 acres of the original Henry Ford Estate. Dearborn is centrally located within one of America's largest business regions. The geographically diverse area provides faculty with a variety of urban, suburban, and rural areas within a reasonable commute, including Detroit, Detroit suburbs, and Ann Arbor. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candid ates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks. Background checks will be performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284637 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - ESE 505 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Dearborn Campus Dearborn, MI Modes of Work Onsite Hybrid Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Dbn Col Engineering Department Dbn Col of Eng-Electric & Comp Posting Begin/End Date 10/27/2026 - 11/06/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
